# 8. BAHRAIN CURRENCY AND VAT

## 8.1 Currency

Use BHD exclusively.

Money must display with exactly three decimal places.

Examples:

```text
BHD 0.500
BHD 1.250
BHD 12.000
```

Use exact decimal arithmetic.

Do not use Float or Double for authoritative monetary calculations.

## 8.2 VAT rates

Default standard VAT rate:

**10%**

Support zero-rated products where legally applicable.

Do not automatically assume every FMCG item is standard-rated.

Product VAT must be explicitly stored in Products.vat_rate.

## 8.3 VAT-inclusive pricing

Treat Products.selling_price as the VAT-inclusive selling price.

Calculate:

```text
gross = quantity × unit_price
net = gross / (1 + vat_rate)
vat = gross - net
```

Apply a documented consistent three-decimal BHD rounding policy at line level.

Persist VAT for every invoice line.

The invoice subtotal is the sum of rounded line net amounts.

The invoice VAT total is the sum of rounded line VAT amounts.

The final invoice total is the sum of line gross totals.

Ensure:

```text
subtotal + vat_total = total
```

## 8.4 Product unit VAT

Products.vat_amount represents derived VAT for one selling unit.

InvoiceLines.vat_amount represents VAT for the entire invoice line.

Do not assume:

```text
line_vat = rounded_unit_vat × quantity
```

Always calculate line VAT from the authoritative line total to avoid accumulated rounding errors.

## 8.5 Calculation example

Product:

```text
Name: Example Item
Selling price: BHD 1.100
VAT rate: 10%
Quantity: 2
```

Expected:

```text
Net amount:   BHD 2.000
VAT:          BHD 0.200
Line total:   BHD 2.200
```

## 8.6 Financial tests

Test:

- Standard-rated products.
- Zero-rated products.
- Fractional quantities.
- Three-decimal unit prices.
- Rounding boundaries.
- Several invoice lines.
- Mixed VAT rates.
- Invoice total reconciliation.
- Historical price preservation.
- Voided invoice exclusion.

